     <section anchor="changes-to-tls" numbered="true" removeInRFC="false" toc="include" pn="section-3">
       <name slugifiedName="name-changes-to-tls">Changes to TLS</name>
       <t indent="0" pn="section-3-1">Because of the deficiencies noted in the previous section:</t>
       <ul bare="false" empty="false" indent="3" spacing="normal" pn="section-3-2">
-        <li pn="section-3-2.1"> TLS clients <bcp14>MUST NOT</bcp14> send the SSL version 2.0 compatible CLIENT-
-   HELLO message format.  Clients <bcp14>MUST NOT</bcp14> send any ClientHello
-   message that specifies a protocol version less than
-   { 0x03, 0x00 }.  As previously stated by the definitions of all
-   previous versions of TLS, the client <bcp14>SHOULD</bcp14> specify the highest
-   protocol version it supports.</li>
+        <li pn="section-3-2.1">TLS clients <bcp14>MUST NOT</bcp14> send the SSL version 2.0 compatible
+CLIENT-HELLO message format.  Clients <bcp14>MUST NOT</bcp14> send any ClientHello
+message that specifies a protocol version less than
+{ 0x03, 0x00 }.  As previously stated by the definitions of all
+previous versions of TLS, the client <bcp14>SHOULD</bcp14> specify the highest
+protocol version it supports.</li>
+        <li pn="section-3-2.2">TLS servers <bcp14>MAY</bcp14> continue to accept ClientHello messages in the
+version 2 CLIENT-HELLO format as specified in RFC 5246 <xref target="TLS1.2" format="default" sectionFormat="of" derivedContent="TLS1.2"/>,
+Appendix E.2.  Note that this does not contradict the prohibition
+against actually negotiating the use of SSL 2.0.</li>
+        <li pn="section-3-2.3">TLS servers <bcp14>MUST NOT</bcp14> reply with an SSL 2.0 SERVER-HELLO with a
+protocol version that is less than { 0x03, 0x00 } and instead <bcp14>MUST</bcp14>
+abort the connection, i.e., when the highest protocol version
+offered by the client is { 0x02, 0x00 }, the TLS connection will
+be refused.</li>
       </ul>
